1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
They hold just about everything we need, from condiments to cargo.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Made of steel, aluminum, paper, and glass.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
They protect and preserve.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
They're underground and above ground, and they journey around the world.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:20,000
They've revolutionized civilization.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, containers on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,620
In China, TVs, blue jeans, and light bulbs are being manufactured by the millions.

8
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:43,240
But how do these goods get to stores in the United States?

9
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,660
And the transport industry's ubiquitous standard shipping containers?

10
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:54,720
To move that enormous bulk from the offshore location of the mainland, they need many, many big boxes.

11
00:00:54,721 --> 00:00:58,820
And the big boxes we speak of are those 45-foot containers

12
00:00:58,821 --> 00:01:02,820
that are traveling down the road with the blue jeans

13
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:06,320
or the televisions or the tires that you see

14
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:09,760
stacked in the shelves in your retail outlets.

15
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:14,400
The container's journey from source to store is a long one.

16
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:17,840
Typically, the goods are stuffed and manufactured

17
00:01:17,841 --> 00:01:20,600
in a distribution center somewhere in China.

18
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:22,420
In this case, we'll say the Guangdong province.

19
00:01:22,421 --> 00:01:25,600
Then from Guangdong to the port of Hong Kong, for example,

20
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,760
that transport is done by one of three ways.

21
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,900
One, over the road via truck.

22
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:32,220
Two, via rail.

23
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:34,960
Or three, via smaller vessels through a river.

24
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:37,460
Then it arrives to the Hong Kong port.

25
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:42,380
Typically between 4,000 containers to about 9,000 for the bigger ships

26
00:01:42,381 --> 00:01:43,980
are loaded at the Hong Kong port.

27
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,420
Then the vessel leaves Hong Kong to the port of L.A., for example.

28
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:52,400
Once arriving at the port of L.A., the container is unloaded.

29
00:01:52,420 --> 00:02:00,320
At that point, the container is loaded onto either a rail or another truck and then transported to its final destination.

30
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:09,580
The container will make about 120 trips from port to port in its approximate 10-year life.

31
00:02:09,581 --> 00:02:13,940
On the journey from Far East to the mainland during typhoon season,

32
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:21,960
there could potentially be 40- and 50-foot waves that rock the vessel itself to very severe pitched angles.

33
00:02:21,961 --> 00:02:28,220
and those containers need to withstand the pounding of the open sea to protect the goods inside.

34
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,900
The containers are designed to hold up to 20 tons of goods,

35
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,960
and they can be carried by three different modes of transportation.

36
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,900
Without this intermodal system capability,

37
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,320
international commerce as we know it would be almost impossible.

38
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,620
The key to the system is the container itself.

39
00:02:46,621 --> 00:02:54,000
At the China International Marine Containers Factory in Shenzhen, China, new containers

40
00:02:54,001 --> 00:02:58,340
are turned out every five minutes.

41
00:02:58,341 --> 00:03:05,180
Huge sheets of steel are folded, bolted, and painted on an assembly line into one of three

42
00:03:05,181 --> 00:03:12,500
lengths as dictated by the International Standards Organization, or ISO.

43
00:03:12,501 --> 00:03:18,260
The uniform lengths are 20 feet, 40 feet and 45 feet.

44
00:03:18,261 --> 00:03:22,260
Most containers today are of the 40-foot variety.

45
00:03:22,261 --> 00:03:26,280
Each container is also manufactured in such a way that they're easily interlocked.

46
00:03:26,281 --> 00:03:31,840
When containers are loaded on vessels, they're connected to one another using a device called

47
00:03:31,841 --> 00:03:32,840
a twist lock.

48
00:03:32,841 --> 00:03:39,380
There's also rod lashing, which is essentially rods that are lashed to the sides of the container

49
00:03:39,381 --> 00:03:42,440
to make them more sturdy when they're stacked on top of one another.

50
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,840
Before containerization, cargo handling took much more time and muscle.

51
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:52,040
The maritime industry is one that's steeped in tradition,

52
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:57,820
and for centuries goods were loaded and unloaded much in the same way.

53
00:03:57,821 --> 00:04:05,560
Freight was moved using cargo nets and grappling hooks to be taken out of cargo holds,

54
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:12,760
at which time a crew of stevedores would then take the freight and rearrange it onto a pallet,

55
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:19,440
which would then be lifted again onto either a carriage or a buggy or even a truck.

56
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:25,180
Then in 1955, Malcolm McClain, a trucking entrepreneur from North Carolina,

57
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:29,160
acquired a steamship company and changed the industry forever.

58
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:34,980
He removed the wheels from his trucks, strengthened the sides of the trailers,

59
00:04:34,981 --> 00:04:36,880
and stacked them on his ship's deck.

60
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:41,560
His prototype container ship, the Ideal X,

61
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:43,440
with 58 trailers on deck,

62
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:47,920
made its inaugural voyage from New Jersey to Texas in 1956.

63
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,500
His enterprise became Sea Land Services,

64
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:53,560
an international shipping company,

65
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,880
and McLean became known as the father of containerization.

66
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:00,500
Around the same time,

67
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:03,040
Oakland-based Mattson Navigation Company

68
00:05:03,041 --> 00:05:05,860
conducted research on ways to control rising costs.

69
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:08,460
It discovered that loading and unloading freight

70
00:05:08,461 --> 00:05:10,780
in the traditional manner was slow and expensive.

71
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:13,780
As a result of this research,

72
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:17,300
Mattson developed its own containerization program,

73
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,740
which effectively revolutionized

74
00:05:19,741 --> 00:05:21,920
the movement of goods in the Pacific Rim.

75
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:26,660
In 1958, Mattson moved its first containers

76
00:05:26,661 --> 00:05:28,340
out of San Francisco Bay.

77
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:32,140
There were 20 containers that moved on Mattson's Hawaiian merchant.

78
00:05:32,141 --> 00:05:39,080
It was an enormous change in the way the Hawaiian economy was serviced from the mainland.

79
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,880
Containerization sparked a waterfront metamorphosis.

80
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:48,560
Crane operators eventually replaced the gangs of longshoremen who once defined the loading docks.

81
00:05:49,460 --> 00:05:54,780
In order to move the heavy steel containers, the gantry crane, or portainer, was developed.

82
00:05:55,240 --> 00:06:00,360
It required only one driver in a cab high above the pier to move cargo from ship to shore.

83
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:03,920
As more shipping companies began using containers,

84
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:07,300
their standardization and interchangeability became crucial

85
00:06:07,301 --> 00:06:09,380
to the success of the intermodal system.

86
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:13,040
All the transport industries had to adapt.

87
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:17,840
For example, for trucks, we had to make the chassis

88
00:06:17,841 --> 00:06:20,880
and the beds of the truck to be able to fit 20 and 40 footers.

89
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:22,800
Rail, we had to do the same thing.

90
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:25,400
We had to make sure that the bridges or any overpasses,

91
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:27,780
at least in the United States, could fit too high.

92
00:06:27,781 --> 00:06:38,100
And for vessels, we had to make sure that the vessel slots that the containers go into are 8 foot wide, 8 and a half foot tall, and obviously 20 or 40 foot.

93
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:44,480
By 2005, approximately 90% of all cargo moved by standardized containers.

94
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:50,080
But as freight containers came to facilitate global trade, the world became a more dangerous place.

95
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:55,480
After 9-11, obviously, security has gone up exponentially.

96
00:06:55,481 --> 00:06:59,320
There have been many noted examples where unauthorized

97
00:06:59,321 --> 00:07:02,420
terrorists have been in a container using that to smuggle

98
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:04,720
a terrorist into the United States.

99
00:07:04,721 --> 00:07:07,420
Another example could be inserting a weapon of mass

100
00:07:07,421 --> 00:07:11,320
destruction into the container itself.

101
00:07:11,321 --> 00:07:14,640
So companies like GE Security have developed a smart

102
00:07:14,641 --> 00:07:18,640
device to secure the container during its vulnerable voyage.

103
00:07:18,641 --> 00:07:21,140
The manner in which we install the container security

104
00:07:21,141 --> 00:07:24,940
device on the already existing 13 million containers is much

105
00:07:24,941 --> 00:07:27,100
much like a Lego piece is installed.

106
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:33,820
The CSD simply snaps on to the door frame of the ISO container.

107
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:38,920
The device has a magnetic sensor that monitors container door openings

108
00:07:38,921 --> 00:07:42,660
and a central processing unit that records the time of those events.

109
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:46,440
The antenna is the only piece that is outside the container.

110
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:51,460
After loading, the shipper accesses the system, logs in an access code,

111
00:07:51,461 --> 00:07:55,000
registers the container number and authorizes sealing the container.

112
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:00,700
In response, the device will transmit a data log

113
00:08:00,701 --> 00:08:03,400
each time the door has been opened and resealed,

114
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:05,600
or whenever it's electronically queried.

115
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:09,780
Before the container arrives at port,

116
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,220
the United States Customs and Border Protection

117
00:08:12,221 --> 00:08:14,380
has analyzed it from a security perspective.

118
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,580
Based upon their risk rules,

119
00:08:18,581 --> 00:08:26,060
they will choose to inspect anywhere from as low as say 1% to upwards around 8 to 10% of those containers,

120
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:29,580
depending on what they feel is the risk profile of that container.

121
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:33,980
One way is via technology to do an x-ray of the box, for example,

122
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:37,140
to see if there are any weapons of mass destruction in it.

123
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:40,420
Another way is to physically open the box and actually check the contents.

124
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:45,800
In the near future, containers with this device may clear customs in far less time,

125
00:08:45,801 --> 00:08:49,260
ultimately getting products to our stores safer and faster.

126
00:08:51,560 --> 00:08:54,920
After the shipping container has been opened at distribution warehouses,

127
00:08:55,540 --> 00:08:57,640
there are usually more containers inside.

128
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:03,100
Like nesting dolls, hundreds of smaller containers fit snugly inside hundreds of boxes,

129
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:05,740
which sit inside the large cargo container.

130
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:11,920
And the wonder material that holds most of the small containers together is corrugated fiberboard.

131
00:09:11,921 --> 00:09:19,760
With paper made from farmed southern pine trees and up to 70% recycled fiber, this

132
00:09:19,761 --> 00:09:25,140
Georgia Pacific factory in Richmond, Indiana produces 65,000 tons of corrugated fiber a

133
00:09:25,141 --> 00:09:26,140
year.

134
00:09:26,141 --> 00:09:32,320
You probably call these cardboard boxes, but the industry begs to differ.

135
00:09:32,321 --> 00:09:37,320
If you look at most boxes or most containers, they used to be made out of layers and layers

136
00:09:37,321 --> 00:09:40,640
of fine paper that makes a board, and they used to call that cardboard.

137
00:09:40,641 --> 00:09:43,240
A lot of packaging was made out of the cardboard back then.

138
00:09:43,241 --> 00:09:45,440
Now there's a lot more product out there

139
00:09:45,441 --> 00:09:46,880
that's made more out of the corrugate

140
00:09:46,881 --> 00:09:50,280
or out of the corrugated sheets that we make.

141
00:09:50,281 --> 00:09:53,660
In 1890, a Brooklyn printer and paper bag maker

142
00:09:53,661 --> 00:09:58,120
named Robert Gare invented the corrugated carton

143
00:09:58,121 --> 00:10:01,160
and ultimately changed the way we pack and ship today.

144
00:10:05,460 --> 00:10:08,740
Today's corrugated boxes are stronger and use less paper

145
00:10:08,741 --> 00:10:11,140
than cardboard boxes.

146
00:10:11,141 --> 00:10:14,240
The boxes we make here are made of three different components.

147
00:10:14,241 --> 00:10:17,020
You have right here, which is what we call the liner part

148
00:10:17,021 --> 00:10:17,721
of the board.

149
00:10:17,722 --> 00:10:20,600
It's a brown, typically looks like a craft paper bag

150
00:10:20,601 --> 00:10:22,020
at a grocery store.

151
00:10:22,021 --> 00:10:26,060
The inside of the box is what we call a medium, which we flute.

152
00:10:26,061 --> 00:10:30,400
It's a very similar process to someone who might be ironing

153
00:10:30,401 --> 00:10:33,400
the wrinkles out of a shirt, but we're ironing wrinkles

154
00:10:33,401 --> 00:10:34,960
into our sheets.

155
00:10:34,961 --> 00:10:37,980
We are taking heat to condition the paper

156
00:10:37,981 --> 00:10:42,100
and steam to make it receptive to forming the flute shapes

157
00:10:42,101 --> 00:10:45,360
and then starch to be the glue or the bond

158
00:10:45,361 --> 00:10:47,860
to take the two separate pieces of paper

159
00:10:47,861 --> 00:10:49,740
and glue them together.

160
00:10:49,741 --> 00:10:54,740
So similar to two sets of gears coming together,

161
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:58,120
the medium will go down in between them

162
00:10:58,121 --> 00:11:00,200
and be formed into the flutes.

163
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:04,680
Architects have known for thousands of years

164
00:11:04,681 --> 00:11:06,560
that an arch with a proper curve

165
00:11:06,561 --> 00:11:09,060
is the strongest way to span a given space.

166
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:13,600
The same principle applies to corrugated fiberboard.

167
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:16,540
Arches in the corrugated medium,

168
00:11:16,541 --> 00:11:18,180
when anchored to the liner board,

169
00:11:18,181 --> 00:11:20,700
resist bending and pressure from all directions.

170
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:24,820
When a piece of combined board is placed on its end,

171
00:11:24,821 --> 00:11:26,960
the arches form rigid columns,

172
00:11:26,961 --> 00:11:29,180
capable of supporting a great deal of weight.

173
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:33,560
When pressure is applied to the side of the board,

174
00:11:33,561 --> 00:11:36,280
the space between the flutes acts as a cushion

175
00:11:36,281 --> 00:11:38,200
to protect the container's contents.

176
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:41,640
After the medium is fluted

177
00:11:41,641 --> 00:11:44,600
in the almost 300 foot long corrugator,

178
00:11:44,601 --> 00:11:47,320
the final layer is glued to the fiberboard.

179
00:11:47,321 --> 00:11:48,600
So then at that point,

180
00:11:48,601 --> 00:11:52,340
you have two of the three substrates joined together

181
00:11:52,341 --> 00:11:55,460
and they will then travel to the other end of the machine

182
00:11:55,461 --> 00:11:58,960
where we will apply a cold glue,

183
00:11:58,961 --> 00:12:00,880
which is a polyvinyl acetate,

184
00:12:00,881 --> 00:12:03,560
very similar to an Elmer's glue,

185
00:12:03,561 --> 00:12:07,280
And we will put another thin adhesion of glue on the flute

186
00:12:07,281 --> 00:12:10,920
tips and then bond the printed liner on top of that.

187
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:16,940
This machine takes the sheets that we just saw laminated.

188
00:12:16,941 --> 00:12:19,860
We feed them through this line one sheet at a time.

189
00:12:19,861 --> 00:12:21,740
They're delivered to a cutting station,

190
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:24,280
where I'm literally cutting them like a cookie cutter.

191
00:12:24,281 --> 00:12:26,000
I've got cutting rule.

192
00:12:26,001 --> 00:12:28,060
I've got creasing rule inside the die

193
00:12:28,061 --> 00:12:29,980
that can actually cut out the board and crease it

194
00:12:29,981 --> 00:12:31,800
where it's supposed to be creased.

195
00:12:31,801 --> 00:12:35,580
It goes on to station two, where we literally strip out the scraps.

196
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:38,580
Scraps are recycled into new boxes immediately.

197
00:12:39,900 --> 00:12:44,480
Once it's stripped, it's delivered to a pallet, and they're taken flat off at the next station.

198
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:50,840
The finished box is folded and glued into various shapes to fit and protect everything from juice to toys.

199
00:12:51,260 --> 00:12:53,040
We can take a blank like this.

200
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:56,200
With the foam and glue, we can turn it into a carton like this.

201
00:12:57,360 --> 00:12:59,380
We can also take a blank in this form.

202
00:12:59,381 --> 00:13:03,140
we can turn it into something that will pack flat in the case for the customer

203
00:13:03,141 --> 00:13:06,740
so that at their end, they can just pop it and fill it.

204
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:10,780
America's corrugated container board is so highly prized

205
00:13:10,781 --> 00:13:16,040
that more than 660,000 tons of it are exported to factories throughout the world

206
00:13:16,041 --> 00:13:17,960
in standardized shipping containers.

207
00:13:19,720 --> 00:13:22,500
Both the containers and the boxes can hold a lot of goods,

208
00:13:22,940 --> 00:13:26,960
but what have you got to store America's petroleum reserve or its food surplus?

209
00:13:26,961 --> 00:13:31,120
Plus, you'll need the biggest containers in the world.

210
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:42,880
In 2004, the value of containerized cargo entering United States ports totaled more than $750 billion.

211
00:13:42,881 --> 00:13:48,880
Containers will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

212
00:13:48,881 --> 00:13:54,948
When it comes to containers, none rival the size of the U.S.

213
00:13:54,949 --> 00:13:57,780
Strategic Petroleum Reserve.

214
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:06,560
Located along the coast of the Gulf of Mexico, the SPR has a capacity of 727 million barrels of oil.

215
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:16,380
The Strategic Petroleum Reserve actually has four different facilities where we store crude oil, two in Louisiana and two in Texas.

216
00:14:16,381 --> 00:14:23,380
The crude oil contained in the SPR is housed in natural underground geologic formations,

217
00:14:23,381 --> 00:14:24,380
salt domes.

218
00:14:24,381 --> 00:14:30,820
A lot of people don't realize the size of salt domes in the Gulf Coast area.

219
00:14:30,821 --> 00:14:34,260
This particular dome is called Brian Mound.

220
00:14:34,261 --> 00:14:38,440
The Brian Mound dome is located approximately 2,000 feet from the surface of the earth,

221
00:14:38,441 --> 00:14:44,760
and at the 2,000-foot contour, it is approximately a mile and a half wide.

222
00:14:44,761 --> 00:14:50,100
In Bryan Mound and other SPR salt domes are massive cylindrical caverns where the crude

223
00:14:50,101 --> 00:14:52,060
oil is stored.

224
00:14:52,061 --> 00:14:56,560
The entire SPR contains 62 of these mammoth caverns.

225
00:14:56,561 --> 00:15:01,640
We currently have 20 caverns here at Bryan Mound with a total capacity of 254 million

226
00:15:01,641 --> 00:15:03,600
barrels subsurface.

227
00:15:03,601 --> 00:15:09,380
If you can imagine the size of the Astrodome, if we were to fill up the Astrodome with crude

228
00:15:09,381 --> 00:15:13,920
oil, it would be equivalent to the amount that we can fill up one of our caverns.

229
00:15:13,921 --> 00:15:20,860
In 1975, President Gerald Ford signed the Energy Policy and Conservation Act, which created the SPR.

230
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:28,260
This was a direct response to the economic crisis generated by the OPEC oil embargo of 1973-74.

231
00:15:29,580 --> 00:15:34,440
The country needed a safety net for future political, economic, and natural disasters

232
00:15:34,441 --> 00:15:38,100
that might threaten the supply of one of America's most vital resources.

233
00:15:38,980 --> 00:15:43,600
The daunting question, where to contain hundreds of millions of barrels of oil?

234
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:48,660
The thoughts that were brought up were above-ground storage, underground storage.

235
00:15:48,661 --> 00:15:55,880
They realized that the Gulf Coast was the most feasible location to store crude oil underground in salt domes.

236
00:15:56,120 --> 00:15:59,480
The majority of the refinery process is located in the Gulf Coast.

237
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:01,960
The pipelines are located in the Gulf Coast.

238
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:07,600
And there were actually some salt domes that already had some leached caverns, solution-mined caverns.

239
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:12,020
Unlike traditional salt mining, which uses machines to dig for the salt,

240
00:16:12,021 --> 00:16:17,020
Solution mining injects millions of gallons of water into salt domes to create caverns.

241
00:16:17,021 --> 00:16:22,020
It's a process in which we use raw water to dissolution the salt,

242
00:16:22,021 --> 00:16:25,020
much like you would dissolve table salt at your house.

243
00:16:25,021 --> 00:16:32,020
And we use approximately seven barrels of raw water to create one barrel of cavern space.

244
00:16:32,021 --> 00:16:39,020
The way that these underground containers are performed or created

245
00:16:39,021 --> 00:16:46,260
created is the introduction or the drilling of two holes approximately 4,000 feet into

246
00:16:46,261 --> 00:16:47,620
the salt dome itself.

247
00:16:47,621 --> 00:16:51,700
Now those holes then have extended casings in it, a pipe within

248
00:16:51,701 --> 00:16:57,700
a pipe, and we introduce water into those pipes, the inner pipe, and begin to dissolve

249
00:16:57,701 --> 00:16:58,401
the salt.

250
00:16:58,402 --> 00:17:02,660
The dissolved salt becomes brine, and the brine comes out the annulus or the

251
00:17:02,661 --> 00:17:04,180
outer pipe.

252
00:17:04,181 --> 00:17:09,660
As the brine is extricated, crude oil takes its place and is stored until needed.

253
00:17:09,661 --> 00:17:14,660
The oil benefits from the natural high temperature subsurface.

254
00:17:14,661 --> 00:17:20,480
The geotechnical heating of the crude oil keeps it at a consistent composition because

255
00:17:20,481 --> 00:17:26,640
we have homogeneity across the crude oil.

256
00:17:26,641 --> 00:17:32,720
In order to extract the oil from its underground reservoir, the SPR performs a drawdown.

257
00:17:32,721 --> 00:17:36,100
pump raw water in through the brine stream to the bottom of the cavern.

258
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:40,520
It displaces the crude oil, which is on top of the brine, and that comes out through a

259
00:17:40,521 --> 00:17:41,221
crude oil header.

260
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:46,280
Oil is lighter than water, so the two substances naturally separate.

261
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:52,340
The oil is then segregated, metered, and distributed to various terminals and refineries.

262
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:58,420
An SPR drawdown only occurs under very specific circumstances.

263
00:17:58,421 --> 00:18:05,020
The president of the United States has authority to release a full-scale drawdown,

264
00:18:05,180 --> 00:18:10,100
which means we release oil from our facilities at 4.4 million barrels a day.

265
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:13,240
He also has the authority to do a limited drawdown,

266
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,760
which allows the release of up to 30 million barrels of crude oil.

267
00:18:17,060 --> 00:18:24,280
The second person that is authorized to release oil from our facilities is the secretary of energy.

268
00:18:24,281 --> 00:18:29,280
There have only been two drawdowns since the SPR was instituted.

269
00:18:29,281 --> 00:18:36,280
A drawdown was authorized after 2005's Hurricane Katrina.

270
00:18:36,281 --> 00:18:42,280
140 mile an hour winds had torn through the Gulf Coast, damaging refineries and pipelines

271
00:18:42,281 --> 00:18:45,280
that feed the entire United States.

272
00:18:45,281 --> 00:18:51,280
And the SPR served the purpose for which it was intended, to ease the stresses of economic,

273
00:18:51,281 --> 00:18:53,880
geopolitical and natural disasters.

274
00:18:54,380 --> 00:18:57,080
We have to be ready when the president so decrees

275
00:18:57,081 --> 00:18:59,020
to be going to full drawdown mode

276
00:18:59,021 --> 00:19:01,240
in which we can supply the country with crude oil.

277
00:19:01,660 --> 00:19:06,080
And we have to be ready like almost the military in that regard.

278
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:09,500
The Strategic Petroleum Reserve

279
00:19:09,501 --> 00:19:12,220
may be the largest underground container in the world.

280
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:13,940
But there are other containers

281
00:19:13,941 --> 00:19:16,900
that hold vast riches above ground, silos.

282
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:21,240
We're standing on the 110-foot-high silo

283
00:19:21,241 --> 00:19:26,180
a 60-foot diameter, and this is the one that holds a quarter

284
00:19:26,181 --> 00:19:26,881
million bushel.

285
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:31,120
A silo's height means it can have a small footprint

286
00:19:31,121 --> 00:19:34,080
and still contain tremendous amounts of whatever needs

287
00:19:34,081 --> 00:19:40,900
storing, like grain, sand, or corn, to name but a few.

288
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,220
Grain elevators and feed plants are comprised of multiple silos.

289
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:49,100
Although a seemingly simple structure,

290
00:19:49,101 --> 00:19:52,020
a silo was actually a very complex edifice.

291
00:19:53,480 --> 00:19:56,760
At the Kroll Family Farm in Lebanon County, Pennsylvania,

292
00:19:57,760 --> 00:20:01,740
construction crew from the Sollenberger Silos Corporation

293
00:20:01,741 --> 00:20:05,580
builds a new farm silo utilizing the jump form method.

294
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:10,340
If you look at the forms, they're four foot high,

295
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:15,580
and there's three four foot forms on the concrete now.

296
00:20:15,960 --> 00:20:18,360
After they finish pouring the concrete in one form,

297
00:20:18,361 --> 00:20:24,580
The crewman will break the forms loose, and then they will push those out and jump them up to the next level.

298
00:20:24,780 --> 00:20:26,260
So that's why we call them jump forms.

299
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:30,040
Pouring the concrete involves precision and speed.

300
00:20:30,700 --> 00:20:36,120
He moves the buggy continuously around so that the concrete fills the form level.

301
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:40,040
He doesn't want to put it all in one place because it'll try to bulge the forms out.

302
00:20:40,560 --> 00:20:42,740
And so he fills it up even all the way around.

303
00:20:43,720 --> 00:20:46,480
Steel rebar supports are placed in the concrete.

304
00:20:46,481 --> 00:20:52,480
Engineers have designed these silos based on the material that's stored.

305
00:20:52,481 --> 00:20:57,480
If we store shell corn, there's a lot of horizontal pressure, and so we need more steel.

306
00:20:57,481 --> 00:21:00,480
That gives us the strength in the silo walls.

307
00:21:00,481 --> 00:21:08,480
When completed, this farm silo will stand 92 feet high and contain 28,888 cubic feet of storage space.

308
00:21:08,481 --> 00:21:12,480
Today's silos are epic versions of their predecessors.

309
00:21:12,481 --> 00:21:20,320
Agricultural scientist Franklin Hiram King patented the first silo in 1889.

310
00:21:22,460 --> 00:21:25,800
King's silo was a modified barn, cylindrical in shape.

311
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:30,780
He knew that the cylinder structure was strong and reduced the likelihood of spoilage.

312
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:37,740
King's silo finally evolved into this behemoth, the largest farm silo in the United States.

313
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:43,020
It stands 148 feet tall in Berks County, Pennsylvania.

314
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:48,620
In this big silo, there's over 90,000 cubic feet of storage capacity.

315
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:55,060
And the Goodyear blimp has about 70,000 cubic feet, so this is bigger than a Goodyear blimp.

316
00:21:57,840 --> 00:22:00,720
For most modern silos, concrete is king.

317
00:22:01,800 --> 00:22:04,480
I always say concrete's for life.

318
00:22:04,481 --> 00:22:09,820
It takes very little maintenance, and you can put just about anything in it.

319
00:22:10,820 --> 00:22:14,140
Silos in the SPR storage tanks are certainly impressive.

320
00:22:14,740 --> 00:22:22,600
But there's another small and humble container we use every day that's changed the course of our world, the metal can.

321
00:22:25,080 --> 00:22:27,416
One storage cavern in the U.S.

322
00:22:27,417 --> 00:22:33,800
Strategic Petroleum Reserve is large enough to contain the 1,450-foot Sears Tower.

323
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:37,820
with room to spare.

324
00:22:37,821 --> 00:22:40,920
Containers will return on Modern Marvels,

325
00:22:40,921 --> 00:22:43,120
here on the History Channel.

326
00:22:43,121 --> 00:22:44,720
On Modern Marvels.

327
00:22:48,660 --> 00:22:53,040
The metal can, one of our most important containers.

328
00:22:53,041 --> 00:22:55,540
This seemingly simple device revolutionized

329
00:22:55,541 --> 00:22:57,640
the food and beverage industry.

330
00:22:57,641 --> 00:22:59,940
The shelves of grocery stores around the world

331
00:22:59,941 --> 00:23:01,780
and our own kitchen cabinets are filled

332
00:23:01,781 --> 00:23:03,980
with every conceivable shape and size.

333
00:23:03,981 --> 00:23:09,940
It's so common that people don't realize how much work and effort goes into making a can.

334
00:23:10,700 --> 00:23:15,880
First of all, the speeds at which cans are made boggle people's minds pretty much.

335
00:23:17,380 --> 00:23:23,700
For the last 125 years, Ball Corporation has been a leader in the food and beverage container industry.

336
00:23:24,620 --> 00:23:29,200
At their Golden, Colorado metal beverage plant, Ball creates billions of perfect cans.

337
00:23:29,540 --> 00:23:33,540
Most can lines are designed to run around 2,000 a minute.

338
00:23:33,541 --> 00:23:38,040
We're making well over $100 million a day just in our plants.

339
00:23:38,760 --> 00:23:43,500
Each year, when you look at our industry, we're making $100 billion.

340
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:50,980
Production begins with 12,000-pound aluminum coils that unspool into a machine called a cupper.

341
00:23:52,620 --> 00:24:02,160
The cupper creates this piece of product that is really the very first process of making a two-piece can.

342
00:24:02,620 --> 00:24:06,000
This process knocks out 12 of these at once,

343
00:24:06,200 --> 00:24:09,420
and these are then conveyed to a machine called the bodymaker.

344
00:24:10,580 --> 00:24:14,900
The bodymaker drives a steel form into the cup, which extends the aluminum.

345
00:24:15,660 --> 00:24:18,000
You can see here that it's untrimmed.

346
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:19,780
It's a little rough on the top.

347
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:24,900
Before the can exits the bodymaker, the top is cut to its standard size,

348
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:26,320
and a pressure dome is created.

349
00:24:26,321 --> 00:24:35,060
It has the pressure dome here, which resists the carbonation from bulging it out.

350
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:40,540
This can is then trimmed to have what we call a factory finish,

351
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:44,060
so that every can has this nice trimmed height,

352
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:47,400
and they're all exactly the same after this point.

353
00:24:48,540 --> 00:24:52,140
Each can then journeys to a system of washers and dryers

354
00:24:52,141 --> 00:24:55,000
that produce clean containers ready for their next phase.

355
00:24:55,001 --> 00:24:56,000
The printer.

356
00:24:56,001 --> 00:25:01,000
The printer is essentially the machine that paces the entire can line.

357
00:25:01,001 --> 00:25:06,600
It'll speed up or slow down and the rest of the can line has to sort of behave in the

358
00:25:06,601 --> 00:25:07,600
same manner.

359
00:25:07,601 --> 00:25:13,640
The printer uses up to eight different colors of ink to design intricate labels.

360
00:25:13,641 --> 00:25:15,960
Then a fine lacquer covers the artwork.

361
00:25:15,961 --> 00:25:19,800
From here, the cans enter an oven that bakes on the ink and lacquer.

362
00:25:19,801 --> 00:25:25,540
And then that can goes from that oven into what we call internal spray machines.

363
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:31,620
And these spray the inside of the can with a coating, and then that is baked on as well.

364
00:25:31,840 --> 00:25:33,820
So you've got no metal exposure.

365
00:25:34,640 --> 00:25:39,880
At each phase of the production line, the cans must be flawless, or else they're discarded and recycled.

366
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:45,220
The final step prior to shipping is to create a neck and flange on the can.

367
00:25:45,221 --> 00:25:48,780
We put a neck and a flange on the can.

368
00:25:48,781 --> 00:25:52,260
This neck and the flanging gives it a lot of strength,

369
00:25:52,261 --> 00:25:54,860
and it prepares it for the end,

370
00:25:54,861 --> 00:25:57,860
which comes on after the can is filled with product.

371
00:25:57,861 --> 00:26:01,500
An end comes on, and this flange is then rolled into

372
00:26:01,501 --> 00:26:04,860
what we call a double seam, which gives it a hermetic seal.

373
00:26:04,861 --> 00:26:08,500
The product is perfectly preserved from light or oxygen,

374
00:26:08,501 --> 00:26:12,940
and nothing comes out of the can either until you open it.

375
00:26:12,941 --> 00:26:15,300
The cans are then aligned onto pallets,

376
00:26:15,301 --> 00:26:17,580
marked for transport, and sent to the warehouse

377
00:26:17,581 --> 00:26:21,180
to await delivery.

378
00:26:21,181 --> 00:26:23,680
But making food and beverage containers hasn't always been

379
00:26:23,681 --> 00:26:26,480
this fast or streamlined.

380
00:26:26,481 --> 00:26:29,520
The sterile container can be traced back to an 18th century

381
00:26:29,521 --> 00:26:32,220
French general, Napoleon Bonaparte.

382
00:26:32,221 --> 00:26:35,740
In 1795, as he was trying to conquer Europe,

383
00:26:35,741 --> 00:26:39,140
he was having a very difficult time obtaining the necessary

384
00:26:39,141 --> 00:26:41,340
foodstuffs for his armies.

385
00:26:41,341 --> 00:26:43,040
And he went back to the French government,

386
00:26:43,041 --> 00:26:46,980
and they actually started a contest to develop a feeding

387
00:26:46,981 --> 00:26:49,980
that would make food non-perishable.

388
00:26:49,981 --> 00:26:52,980
And a fellow by the name of Nicholas Apare in 1795

389
00:26:52,981 --> 00:26:54,980
took on the project.

390
00:26:54,981 --> 00:26:58,980
It took almost 15 years, but Nicholas Apare was successful.

391
00:26:58,981 --> 00:27:01,980
He partially cooked food, put it in a glass container,

392
00:27:01,981 --> 00:27:03,980
corked it, and then boiled the bottle.

393
00:27:03,981 --> 00:27:06,980
Englishman Peter Durand had a different idea.

394
00:27:06,981 --> 00:27:09,980
He started working with metal instead of glass

395
00:27:09,981 --> 00:27:10,980
in the early 1800s.

396
00:27:10,981 --> 00:27:13,980
And he figured out a way to use the metal can

397
00:27:13,981 --> 00:27:16,980
as opposed to glass, doing basically

398
00:27:16,981 --> 00:27:22,560
the same process, but the metal can was much more durable, it didn't break, and the result was that

399
00:27:22,561 --> 00:27:27,601
Duran's process was much more sterile and had more longevity.

400
00:27:27,602 --> 00:27:30,080
Duran combated rust buildup in

401
00:27:30,081 --> 00:27:33,451
his iron cans with a tin lining that protected the food.

402
00:27:33,452 --> 00:27:36,040
He then soldered on a lid after the can was

403
00:27:36,041 --> 00:27:39,651
filled. That was the dawn of the tin can.

404
00:27:39,652 --> 00:27:44,760
Duran's innovation soon found many practical applications,

405
00:27:44,761 --> 00:27:47,300
including the preservation of fruit and vegetables,

406
00:27:47,580 --> 00:27:50,360
which could now retain their vital nutritional value.

407
00:27:52,740 --> 00:27:56,000
Scurvy, which had been the bane of many an English seaman, ended.

408
00:27:56,980 --> 00:27:58,880
Sailors who had once starved for nutrients

409
00:27:58,881 --> 00:28:00,700
could now withstand long voyages.

410
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:04,940
By the time of the American Civil War,

411
00:28:05,460 --> 00:28:08,160
Union soldiers relied on canned food for survival

412
00:28:08,161 --> 00:28:09,860
almost as much as gunpowder.

413
00:28:09,861 --> 00:28:12,780
It became something that allowed civilization

414
00:28:12,781 --> 00:28:16,740
to do things other than just forage for their food.

415
00:28:17,160 --> 00:28:20,320
So the preservation of food in a safe manner

416
00:28:20,321 --> 00:28:23,160
allowed them to begin to do other things.

417
00:28:23,440 --> 00:28:26,180
And, you know, that was really the beginning of industrialization.

418
00:28:26,940 --> 00:28:28,200
In the decades to follow,

419
00:28:28,760 --> 00:28:31,380
automation caused the tin can industry to flourish.

420
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:33,600
During the Second World War,

421
00:28:33,700 --> 00:28:37,340
the can's value became deeply rooted in the minds of American GIs.

422
00:28:37,700 --> 00:28:41,220
Two-thirds of the food served to the soldiers in the Second World War

423
00:28:41,221 --> 00:28:42,060
was canned food.

424
00:28:42,061 --> 00:28:46,740
And then they came home, and families were dispersing and going elsewhere,

425
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:50,460
and the result was that they had a real confidence in canned food.

426
00:28:50,820 --> 00:28:56,340
In the post-war era, food in a can became a staple in every home and propelled the industry forward.

427
00:28:57,060 --> 00:29:04,960
In North America, today we process 33.5 to 34 billion cans of food every single year.

428
00:29:05,320 --> 00:29:08,200
The metal container has been threatened by other forms of packaging,

429
00:29:08,201 --> 00:29:11,400
plastic and flexible packaging and things like that.

430
00:29:11,401 --> 00:29:16,460
And I can honestly tell you that I've seen the obituary written for the metal can several times.

431
00:29:17,020 --> 00:29:20,820
But when all is said and done, the metal container is still going to survive.

432
00:29:23,120 --> 00:29:27,320
While the metal can may hold the distinction as the world's most important container,

433
00:29:27,960 --> 00:29:32,780
another can with a clever innovation nearly eclipsed its importance, the aerosol can.

434
00:29:33,420 --> 00:29:36,600
The aerosol can came into its own in the 1940s.

435
00:29:37,320 --> 00:29:40,660
Again, war proved to be the impetus for significant innovation.

436
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:42,100
The U.S.

437
00:29:42,101 --> 00:29:46,940
government funded research for a portable container that soldiers could easily

438
00:29:46,941 --> 00:29:52,820
carry and use to spray disease-carrying insects.

439
00:29:52,821 --> 00:29:56,920
This device proved invaluable for the troops in the Pacific Theater, where malaria was

440
00:29:56,921 --> 00:29:59,820
rampant.

441
00:29:59,821 --> 00:30:04,980
This insecticide spray is credited with saving thousands of soldiers' lives.

442
00:30:04,981 --> 00:30:09,980
The proven success of the aerosol can in war soon expanded to the home front, where families

443
00:30:09,981 --> 00:30:16,820
applied everything from hairspray to medicine with increasing ease.

444
00:30:16,821 --> 00:30:24,920
An aerosol can is made of steel or aluminum and works with the use of a propellant.

445
00:30:24,921 --> 00:30:26,760
The propellant is a gas.

446
00:30:26,761 --> 00:30:31,200
When the nozzle at the top is depressed, the force of the gas pushing down on the product

447
00:30:31,201 --> 00:30:36,360
causes it to move through the dip tube and finally exit the nozzle.

448
00:30:36,361 --> 00:30:40,960
The narrow nozzle atomizes the liquid and forms a fine mist.

449
00:30:40,961 --> 00:30:49,320
Early aerosol cans used liquefied gas that contained chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs.

450
00:30:49,321 --> 00:30:55,080
But scientists discovered that CFCs caused detrimental effects on the ozone layer.

451
00:30:55,081 --> 00:31:00,120
They were removed from more than 90% of all aerosols in the U.S. by 1978.

452
00:31:00,121 --> 00:31:07,240
In the 1980s, CFCs were banned worldwide, but manufacturers soon devised harmless propellants.

453
00:31:08,220 --> 00:31:12,200
Today's aerosol industry manufactures over 3 billion containers each year.

454
00:31:12,780 --> 00:31:22,040
And although the can sealed its place in society, a resealable plastic container put the lid on fresh food, changing our kitchens forever.

455
00:31:22,041 --> 00:31:32,760
A sealed tin container of roasted veal from one of Sir William Perry's Arctic expeditions in the 1820s was opened in 1938.

456
00:31:33,460 --> 00:31:36,940
The contents were then fed to a cat with no ill effects.

457
00:31:37,400 --> 00:31:39,940
Containers will return on Modern Marvels.

458
00:31:41,220 --> 00:31:47,560
In the United States, 36 billion glass containers are produced per year.

459
00:31:48,280 --> 00:31:50,420
They'll hold everything from baby food to pickles.

460
00:31:52,040 --> 00:31:52,792
At the O.I.

461
00:31:52,793 --> 00:31:57,780
factory in Vernon, California, they make one million glass bottles a day.

462
00:31:59,680 --> 00:32:03,080
The six-step manufacturing process starts with raw materials.

463
00:32:04,180 --> 00:32:06,060
This is the batch house facility.

464
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:10,000
As you can see, there's a number of silos here where we store our raw materials.

465
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:16,300
Silica sand, limestone, soda ash, and hullet, which is our recycled glass.

466
00:32:16,301 --> 00:32:21,420
And within this facility, we weigh it, we mix it, and we then deliver it to the furnaces

467
00:32:21,421 --> 00:32:24,560
via a conveyor system.

468
00:32:24,561 --> 00:32:28,020
The fiery furnace that is approximately five feet deep

469
00:32:28,021 --> 00:32:31,500
converts the raw materials into molten glass.

470
00:32:31,501 --> 00:32:35,460
This furnace consists of high temperature ceramic brick

471
00:32:35,461 --> 00:32:38,600
of many different sizes and shapes and weights.

472
00:32:38,601 --> 00:32:42,340
It contains about 500 tons of molten glass

473
00:32:42,341 --> 00:32:45,520
at about 2,800 degrees.

474
00:32:45,521 --> 00:32:47,680
After the glass is melted in the furnace,

475
00:32:47,681 --> 00:32:49,940
it's delivered to the forming machine.

476
00:32:49,941 --> 00:32:52,900
The molten glass gets sheared into uniform pieces called

477
00:32:52,901 --> 00:32:58,740
gauze and then blown into the mold shape with compressed air.

478
00:32:58,741 --> 00:33:01,540
The glass enters the forming machine at 2,100 degrees

479
00:33:01,541 --> 00:33:04,340
Fahrenheit.

480
00:33:04,341 --> 00:33:07,300
Formed glass containers leave the machine at 900 degrees

481
00:33:07,301 --> 00:33:09,780
Fahrenheit in less than 10 seconds.

482
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:12,340
This rapid cooling helps the glass retain its shape.

483
00:33:15,780 --> 00:33:18,500
The extreme change in temperature can cause stress

484
00:33:18,501 --> 00:33:23,140
in the bottles if they're not cooled properly.

485
00:33:23,141 --> 00:33:26,100
Therefore, the formed containers are loaded into an annealing

486
00:33:26,101 --> 00:33:30,020
leer where their temperature is raised to 1,000 degrees, then

487
00:33:30,021 --> 00:33:33,620
reduced gradually to below 200 degrees.

488
00:33:33,621 --> 00:33:36,260
This reheating and slow cooling eliminates the stress in the

489
00:33:36,261 --> 00:33:40,820
containers, making them stronger and more shock resistant.

490
00:33:40,821 --> 00:33:44,020
After the annealing process is complete and the bottles have

491
00:33:44,021 --> 00:33:47,620
cooled down to about 200 degrees Fahrenheit, we apply a

492
00:33:47,621 --> 00:33:49,620
coating on the outside of the bottles.

493
00:33:49,621 --> 00:33:52,620
The coating protects the bottles against abrasions

494
00:33:52,621 --> 00:33:55,620
and scratches, thereby enhancing the strength characteristics

495
00:33:55,621 --> 00:33:58,620
of the container.

496
00:33:58,621 --> 00:34:00,620
Each bottle is inspected.

497
00:34:00,621 --> 00:34:03,620
Those that don't pass get sent back to the fire.

498
00:34:03,621 --> 00:34:07,620
After inspection, some of our bottles are bulk palletized

499
00:34:07,621 --> 00:34:09,620
rather than being put into cartons.

500
00:34:09,621 --> 00:34:13,620
This machine right here puts our bottles on top of a pallet

501
00:34:13,621 --> 00:34:15,620
nine layers high.

502
00:34:15,621 --> 00:34:20,140
We then strap them to the pallet in preparation for shipment to the customer.

503
00:34:21,020 --> 00:34:26,860
The highly mechanized system that runs 24-7 and over 81 OI plants worldwide

504
00:34:26,861 --> 00:34:31,300
turn raw materials into billions of transparent, strong containers a year.

505
00:34:32,300 --> 00:34:36,880
It's taken glassmakers thousands of years to reach this level of speed and efficiency.

506
00:34:38,080 --> 00:34:44,040
Around 1500 B.C., Egyptian craftsmen developed a method for producing the first glass pots

507
00:34:44,041 --> 00:34:48,600
by dipping a core mold of compacted sand into molten glass

508
00:34:48,601 --> 00:34:51,720
and then turning the mold so that the glass adhered to it.

509
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:56,520
While still soft, the glass-covered mold was rolled on a slab of stone

510
00:34:56,521 --> 00:34:58,700
in order to smooth or decorate it.

511
00:35:00,180 --> 00:35:04,540
Then sometime between 27 B.C. and 14 A.D. in Syria,

512
00:35:04,541 --> 00:35:07,800
the blowing of glass transformed glass container making.

513
00:35:09,860 --> 00:35:12,940
Glass blowing has basically remained unchanged.

514
00:35:13,500 --> 00:35:15,520
Once the glass is heated into a semi-liquid,

515
00:35:15,920 --> 00:35:18,460
it's placed at the end of a rod called a punty.

516
00:35:19,840 --> 00:35:22,160
The glass blower then blows into the punty

517
00:35:22,161 --> 00:35:24,320
to shape the glass into different formed containers.

518
00:35:25,700 --> 00:35:29,680
By 1880, more than 25% of the glass made in America

519
00:35:29,681 --> 00:35:31,220
was used for common bottles

520
00:35:31,221 --> 00:35:33,600
that were arduously blown one at a time.

521
00:35:36,060 --> 00:35:38,320
In 1903, all that changed

522
00:35:38,321 --> 00:35:41,240
with an invention by a young glass blower from Ohio.

523
00:35:41,241 --> 00:35:46,880
The first fully automatic bottle machine invented by Mike Owens would make nine bottles

524
00:35:46,881 --> 00:35:47,880
per minute.

525
00:35:47,881 --> 00:35:53,000
And to put that in perspective, the output of that machine in one day would have required

526
00:35:53,001 --> 00:35:57,640
36 men and boys working for 18 hours blowing bottles by hand.

527
00:35:57,641 --> 00:36:00,620
High speed filling and packing line soon followed.

528
00:36:00,621 --> 00:36:05,680
The O in OI stands for Owens, and it's the legacy of his machine on which the company

529
00:36:05,681 --> 00:36:08,400
has built its global container business.

530
00:36:08,401 --> 00:36:13,640
It continues to produce billions of glass containers every year.

531
00:36:13,641 --> 00:36:18,480
Before the post-war era in the 1940s, glass and metal containers were the most common

532
00:36:18,481 --> 00:36:21,140
options available to store food.

533
00:36:21,141 --> 00:36:26,000
That is until a man named Earl Tupper revolutionized food storage and preparation.

534
00:36:26,001 --> 00:36:33,680
Well, before Earl Tupper came along, you know, this was still a time before most refrigerators,

535
00:36:33,681 --> 00:36:36,780
many people were still having ice delivered to their home.

536
00:36:36,781 --> 00:36:41,220
containers were basically designed to store for a day or so.

537
00:36:41,221 --> 00:36:46,160
In the 1940s, the plastic industry was still in its infancy.

538
00:36:46,161 --> 00:36:50,280
Plastic containers were difficult to market because they were known to be brittle, greasy,

539
00:36:50,281 --> 00:36:52,960
smelly and generally unreliable.

540
00:36:52,961 --> 00:36:58,680
Therefore, Tupper developed two important innovations in plastic technology.

541
00:36:58,681 --> 00:37:04,520
He helped to develop the process of refining polyethylene slag.

542
00:37:04,521 --> 00:37:11,140
slag is essentially this black substance that is a byproduct of oil refinement in the petroleum

543
00:37:11,141 --> 00:37:11,841
industry.

544
00:37:11,842 --> 00:37:17,840
Through this process, he was able to develop a material that was very durable,

545
00:37:17,841 --> 00:37:21,205
that was flexible, great for food conservation.

546
00:37:21,206 --> 00:37:24,140
It was a material that was non-porous and

547
00:37:24,141 --> 00:37:25,382
even translucent.

548
00:37:25,383 --> 00:37:30,640
And he developed the Tupper Seal, an airtight and watertight lid modeled

549
00:37:30,641 --> 00:37:32,140
on a common hardware store item.

550
00:37:33,080 --> 00:37:37,800
He was in a hardware store one day, and he saw a paint can,

551
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:39,880
and he noticed that the paint can, you know,

552
00:37:39,920 --> 00:37:42,380
there was a seal in the center of the paint can.

553
00:37:43,040 --> 00:37:49,340
And he got the idea, if I made the seal go around the outer edges of a bowl,

554
00:37:49,580 --> 00:37:54,580
this would be compatible with a need that was coming of age

555
00:37:54,581 --> 00:37:58,100
as people were buying refrigerators in the post-war years.

556
00:37:58,101 --> 00:38:04,060
The seal was able to seal in things like moisture so that the food did not dry out.

557
00:38:04,280 --> 00:38:10,800
It kept the food from wilting, basically maintained the overall taste and texture of the food as well for long-term conservation.

558
00:38:11,240 --> 00:38:16,460
With housewives demonstrating the revolutionary seal at Tupperware parties, sales skyrocketed.

559
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:21,160
Soon everyone's macaroni and egg salads were kept fresh in Tupperware containers.

560
00:38:22,200 --> 00:38:27,160
Tupperware and other container manufacturers needed to develop new technology in the late 1970s.

561
00:38:28,100 --> 00:38:30,000
when the microwave oven entered the kitchen.

562
00:38:30,880 --> 00:38:34,100
But it was the TV dinner container that would have to change the most.

563
00:38:34,920 --> 00:38:39,600
The conventional aluminum tray and the microwave oven were a particularly bad match.

564
00:38:39,900 --> 00:38:44,260
They didn't have anything inside the oven cavity to absorb the microwave energy.

565
00:38:44,780 --> 00:38:49,140
So if you put metal in, it reflected back to what we call the magnetron.

566
00:38:49,340 --> 00:38:51,080
That's what generates the microwaves.

567
00:38:51,081 --> 00:38:54,000
The waves would reflect back, heat it up, and burn it out.

568
00:38:58,100 --> 00:39:04,100
1986 saw the first microwave oven-safe plastic and paperboard containers.

569
00:39:04,101 --> 00:39:10,100
Today, about 2 billion meals in microwavable containers are sold annually in the United States.

570
00:39:12,100 --> 00:39:19,100
With technology changing the look and use of containers, designers constantly have to think outside of the box.

571
00:39:19,101 --> 00:39:22,100
In London, two developers have done just that.

572
00:39:22,101 --> 00:39:26,100
And now, people are living inside the box.

573
00:39:26,101 --> 00:39:31,100
Glass can be recycled an infinite number of times.

574
00:39:31,101 --> 00:39:35,100
Recycling just one glass bottle saves enough electricity

575
00:39:35,101 --> 00:39:39,100
to light a 100-watt bulb for four hours.

576
00:39:39,101 --> 00:39:42,100
Containers will return on Modern Marvels,

577
00:39:42,101 --> 00:39:45,100
here on the History Channel.

578
00:39:49,100 --> 00:39:52,100
Enough shipping containers exist on our planet

579
00:39:52,101 --> 00:39:55,100
to build an 8-foot-high wall around the equator twice

580
00:39:55,101 --> 00:40:00,860
twice. For the containers that have completed their tour of duty at sea, a

581
00:40:00,861 --> 00:40:06,740
visionary group of developers have found a second life for them. Eldon Scott and

582
00:40:06,741 --> 00:40:11,660
Eric Reynolds of Urban Space Management built Container City in the heart of

583
00:40:11,661 --> 00:40:17,120
London's Docklands area. Container City looks, it looks like Lego bricks, sort of

584
00:40:17,121 --> 00:40:21,440
red, orange and yellow bricks stacked on top of each other in a seemingly quite

585
00:40:21,441 --> 00:40:26,420
random style. They have these little balconies which are also made up of

586
00:40:26,421 --> 00:40:32,420
containers and railings across the front. A container's final destination is

587
00:40:32,421 --> 00:40:36,060
usually the scrapyard, but these containers were destined for greater

588
00:40:36,061 --> 00:40:41,200
things. The cheapest form of construction is an existing box, which is what the

589
00:40:41,201 --> 00:40:44,865
container is. The container supplies, when you buy it, everything.

590
00:40:44,866 --> 00:40:45,920
It's got the floor,

591
00:40:45,921 --> 00:40:50,880
the walls, and the roof. Apart from that, they're very strong. They're quite capable

592
00:40:50,881 --> 00:40:54,320
been put into quite funky arrangements.

593
00:40:54,321 --> 00:40:56,980
Scott and Reynolds converted the bulky cargo containers

594
00:40:56,981 --> 00:41:00,920
into comfortable live workspaces.

595
00:41:00,921 --> 00:41:02,160
When people hear that you live in a container,

596
00:41:02,161 --> 00:41:07,000
they think you're living in a drippy, awful, cold tin can.

597
00:41:07,001 --> 00:41:09,560
And they say, well, the winter's coming.

598
00:41:09,561 --> 00:41:11,040
You're going to be terribly cold.

599
00:41:11,041 --> 00:41:13,140
I think, no, they're insulated.

600
00:41:13,141 --> 00:41:14,760
You know, we have electricity, we have running water.

601
00:41:14,761 --> 00:41:16,340
You know, it's actually very civilized.

602
00:41:16,341 --> 00:41:18,280
To keep costs low, the architects

603
00:41:18,281 --> 00:41:21,280
used 100% recycled materials.

604
00:41:21,281 --> 00:41:24,880
An international standard container is exactly that.

605
00:41:24,881 --> 00:41:27,320
They are precisely within a couple of millimeters,

606
00:41:27,321 --> 00:41:30,400
the same size at the corners, because all those corners

607
00:41:30,401 --> 00:41:32,200
join together.

608
00:41:32,201 --> 00:41:34,040
So that's a wonderful thing about building with them,

609
00:41:34,041 --> 00:41:37,400
of course, that you don't have the problem you do with, say,

610
00:41:37,401 --> 00:41:41,720
bricks or blocks, which come in slightly different sizes,

611
00:41:41,721 --> 00:41:43,080
and you have to make it up with cement.

612
00:41:43,081 --> 00:41:45,800
Containers you know are going to be perfect.

613
00:41:45,801 --> 00:41:48,240
so you can buy them anywhere and they will work.

614
00:41:50,420 --> 00:41:53,620
It took five months to erect Container City One,

615
00:41:53,621 --> 00:41:57,440
which opened in May 2001 and provided 12 work studios.

616
00:41:58,960 --> 00:42:02,680
In 2003, another floor was added in less than two days

617
00:42:02,681 --> 00:42:05,460
and provided three additional live-work apartments.

618
00:42:06,520 --> 00:42:09,880
A total of 100 units now make up Container City.

619
00:42:10,920 --> 00:42:13,760
There was enough demand, and again, to prove a point,

620
00:42:13,761 --> 00:42:16,360
because these things are designed to stack,

621
00:42:16,361 --> 00:42:19,100
they literally pop on top of each other,

622
00:42:19,101 --> 00:42:20,820
we put another floor on it.

623
00:42:21,940 --> 00:42:25,600
A 300 square foot space, the size of one container,

624
00:42:25,601 --> 00:42:28,920
costs about 80 to $140 per month to rent.

625
00:42:31,680 --> 00:42:33,860
The success of Container City led to a boom

626
00:42:33,861 --> 00:42:35,980
in other container buildings for schools,

627
00:42:35,981 --> 00:42:37,680
hospitals, and sports centers.

628
00:42:39,180 --> 00:42:42,740
We have accidentally found ourselves

629
00:42:42,741 --> 00:42:44,780
actually running a business making these things.

630
00:42:44,781 --> 00:42:46,840
They were initially for ourselves.

631
00:42:46,841 --> 00:42:49,440
Urban space management has gone global

632
00:42:49,441 --> 00:42:51,420
with their livable containers.

633
00:42:51,421 --> 00:42:53,700
They're making sales in South America,

634
00:42:53,701 --> 00:42:55,960
Pakistan, and Australia.

635
00:42:58,760 --> 00:43:01,260
However, they might have competition in Australia.

636
00:43:02,360 --> 00:43:06,000
There, architect Sean Godsell is turning shipping containers

637
00:43:06,001 --> 00:43:07,920
into emergency and relief housing

638
00:43:07,921 --> 00:43:10,400
for people who are displaced by disasters,

639
00:43:10,401 --> 00:43:11,700
like Hurricane Katrina.

640
00:43:12,740 --> 00:43:20,580
His concept, named Future Shack, is designed to be mass-produced, inexpensive, and easy to ship and stockpile.

641
00:43:21,580 --> 00:43:25,460
The shacks are completely self-contained and can be erected in 24 hours.

642
00:43:26,180 --> 00:43:30,320
The inside is lined with plywood walls that contain built-in furniture.

643
00:43:31,220 --> 00:43:34,080
There is also a separating wall that houses plumbing necessities.

644
00:43:35,780 --> 00:43:38,100
Gonsal added only a few exterior changes.

645
00:43:38,560 --> 00:43:42,220
Several additional slots used to secure the structure to a foundation.

646
00:43:42,740 --> 00:43:44,860
a top-hinged front opening for the entrance,

647
00:43:45,420 --> 00:43:48,560
and a series of operable panels in the roof for ventilation.

648
00:43:50,140 --> 00:43:53,460
Despite the alterations, the shack maintains its seaworthiness

649
00:43:53,461 --> 00:43:56,140
and its ability to be shipped anywhere in the world,

650
00:43:56,620 --> 00:43:58,560
just like its unmodified predecessor.

651
00:44:00,940 --> 00:44:03,180
Architects and city planners across the globe

652
00:44:03,181 --> 00:44:05,200
are taking note of these modular ideas.

653
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:09,940
Homes, offices, studios, and vital infrastructure

654
00:44:09,941 --> 00:44:14,320
soon may be shipped into a neighborhood near you.

655
00:44:14,321 --> 00:44:20,320
After all, a good new container is never out of style.

